Separate eventual confirmation from delay and throughput guarantees (@computing-communication-progress: 0, is #chapter, #hephaestus-core "Investigate-framing-retries-and-independently-checked-remote-effects.lingua#r_0026D1A44A0804C35C72987DCD") { r_00DA585198FFD77571D6EDD999
Safety survives arbitrary message loss in our declared model: a client can wait forever without producing an incorrect confirmation. Progress requires more. Assume a finite representable workload, eventual endpoint recovery with the durable state intact, continued retries while a request is outstanding, and a channel/service premise that a message transmitted repeatedly eventually reaches a receiving step. Apply that premise separately to requests and replies, after every finite execution prefix. It concerns successful delivery and processing, not merely giving each process CPU time.

Fix an outstanding identity i. If it is not yet committed, repeated requests eventually reach the receiver and commit it. If it was already committed, a duplicate yields its retained result. Until the client accepts i, it sends no i+1, so the server cannot replace i's cached result. If confirmation were never received, retries would continue, infinitely many would yield replies under the repeated-delivery premise, and eventually a reply would reach the client, a contradiction. The client therefore confirms i. Induction over the finite jobs proves eventual completion. It does not prove a uniform bound on waiting time.

This premise excludes a permanent partition, permanent peer failure and an execution that drops every reply. Fair scheduling of endpoints alone permits all of those network histories. The explorer checks that every reachable state has some path to completion; it also retains crash and retransmission self-loops. Reverse reachability demonstrates possible completion, while the conditional argument establishes completion for the stipulated fair executions. A finite graph with a route to success can still contain infinite runs that never take it.

Introduce a separate ideal timing model only after these distinctions. Suppose requests contain five transmitted bytes, replies six, both links serialize at B bytes per second, each direction has propagation delay d seconds, server processing takes p seconds, and the client waits for each reply before sending the next request. With no losses, no queuing and no overlap between operations, cycle time is 11/B+2d+p seconds. For B=1000, d=0.020 and p=0.004, this is 0.055 seconds, giving about 18.18 completed operations per second. Useful amount bytes per second are also 18.18 here, but accepted pieces per second depend on the amounts. This accounting excludes all lower-layer headers and is not an Internet benchmark.

If pipelining is introduced, multiple operations can be in flight and the earlier one-outstanding proof must change. More capacity cannot be inferred by just dividing the same cycle by a chosen window: receiver ordering, result retention, buffering, congestion and loss recovery matter. Likewise, retrying more often can increase offered load without increasing useful completions. Name the resource and time basis when discussing throughput, latency and queued work.

For another deliberately separate model, assume independent rounds, each with request success probability u and conditional reply success probability v, no residual delayed messages between rounds, and the same durable duplicate suppression. A complete round succeeds with s=uv. Then Pr(N>n)=(1−s)^n and E[N]=Σₙ≥₀Pr(N>n)=1/s for 0<s≤1, by the geometric series. For u=3/4 and v=2/3, the mean is two rounds. The stochastic model has stronger assumptions than the arbitrary-loss explorer; neither its probabilities nor timing values can be estimated from state counts.

**Work:** reuse [Separate safety from progress and account for waiting](Separate-safety-from-progress-and-account-for-waiting.lingua#r_00BBBFDC1DF17364A8D5372BB8) and [Review continuous models estimation and the scope of coverage](../../inquiry/core/Review-continuous-models-estimation-and-the-scope-of-coverage.lingua#r_0092AAD67F9A97EFCB0180340A) for conditions on progress and probabilistic models. Construct any desired finite delay before eventual success without violating the qualitative delivery premise. Recalculate the ideal cycle after doubling B and explain why propagation still limits improvement. Challenge the independent-round model with correlated outages and retained delayed replies, then specify what additional observations a real performance claim would require.

**Continue:** [Investigate framing retries and independently checked remote effects](Investigate-framing-retries-and-independently-checked-remote-effects.lingua#r_0026D1A44A0804C35C72987DCD).

